Session transcripts record every command the agent runs, so an ordinary
`git remote -v` banks a credential-bearing URL on disk permanently. Measured on
this repo: 584 occurrences of four live .env credentials across 35 retained
files, in four stores no session hook can reach:

  27 gzipped archives            384
  .agent/.training-data.jsonl    122   (1.19 GB, maintained for finetuning)
  .deprecated-datasets/           78
  live + demoted slots            12
  PID-less orphan (Jul 12)         8
  current session                 12

The training-data corpus matters most: a credential there is on a path into
model weights. The PID-less orphan is structurally unreachable by the existing
orphan sweep, which iterates .transcript-sync.<sid>.pid files and derives the
transcript name from them -- a transcript whose PID file is gone is invisible
forever. The backfill globs transcripts instead.

WHAT THIS SHIPS
- .agent/hooks/_shared/scrub_secrets.py -- the redaction primitive.
- scripts/scrub-transcript-corpus -- the backfill. Dry-run by DEFAULT; --apply
  required. Gzip members swap in only after the replacement validates as a
  non-empty stream.
- .secrets_for_scrubbing_upon_archive.txt gitignored, with a committed .example.
  .agent/.scrub-* gitignored (atomic-replace temps hold transcript content).

WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT SHIP: THE HOOK WIRING.
An earlier revision wired the scrubber into rotate-on-session-end.sh and
launch-transcript-sync.sh. Three adversarial reviews independently refuted the
resulting claim, and two demonstrated DATA LOSS I introduced: the old pipeline
was `gzip -c <readable file>`, which essentially cannot fail, so inserting a
fallible Python process between the bytes and their only copy converted "archive
succeeded" into "archive is a 20-byte empty gzip and the source is gone" -- at
exit 0, with 2>/dev/null swallowing the traceback. It also turned an existing
in-tree test red (test_watcher_lifecycle::test_orphan_sweep_archives_dead_
session_files), which I had not run. Both hook files are reverted to HEAD; that
test is green. Wiring is a follow-up requiring fail-safe archive writes (temp +
gzip -t + rename, source removed only after validation), --repo-root at every
call site, orphan sweep by glob not PID, and an end-to-end test -- no existing
rotation test would notice if the scrubber did nothing at all.

DESIGN, EACH POINT FROM A DEMONSTRATED DEFECT
- Binary, chunked, overlap-carrying; never decodes. The first version used text
  mode with errors="replace" and universal newlines: it rewrote non-UTF-8 bytes
  to U+FFFD, collapsed CRLF, and split a record on a lone CR -- with NO secret
  present. Being line-based it also let a secret straddling a \r evade redaction
  AND rewrote the \r, making that credential permanently immune to re-scrubbing.
  Chunking fixes the mutation, the newline translation, the straddle evasion and
  the memory profile together.
- Conservative .env harvesting. A false positive is worse than a miss: a miss
  leaves one credential in a gitignored file; redacting an ordinary string
  rewrites the corpus irreversibly. A plausible CACHE_KEY=packages/hypergumbo-core
  would have replaced 376,928 occurrences across 265 files. So a bare *_KEY/*_PAT
  suffix is rejected AND the value must be credential-shaped. The 16-char floor
  applies to explicit entries too.
- Deterministic (-length, lexicographic) ordering + fixpoint replacement:
  length alone left a different fragment per run (set iteration order).
- Inline `# comment` stripped from .env values -- folding it in meant the real
  token never matched while the tool reported clean.
- ns-precision mtime (rotation reads it), symlinks resolved, hard links warned,
  group ownership restored so a privacy tool cannot widen a transcript's audience
  under a setgid directory.
- No secret ever reaches stdout, stderr, a log or a filename; counts and .env
  KEY names only, with a test asserting it.

TESTING NOTE, HONESTLY. smart-test SKIPPED this change entirely ("No Python
source files changed") because nothing here lives under packages/*/src -- so the
per-PR gate is structurally blind to .agent/hooks/ and scripts/. I ran the
top-level suite directly: 1387 passed, 3 failed. All 3 pre-exist on clean
origin/dev (verified in a detached worktree): test_check_edge_type_drift x2 and
test_contribute_github x1. scrub_secrets.py is at 100% coverage over 35 tests.
.ci/affected-tests.txt is left unchanged on purpose -- a regenerated marker
would assert per-PR coverage this change does not have. Filing the smart-test
blindness separately; it is a scripts/smart-test change and does not belong in
this PR.

Rotating the credential remains the actual remediation: scrubbing cannot
un-disclose a value that has already been on disk. --apply has NOT been run.

Governance: touches .agent/** and .gitignore. Human approval given explicitly,
including self-merge without --gov.

